DEEP[1]=("Chapter 11 is named Viśvarūpa-Darśana Yoga, the yoga of the vision of the universal form. It has 55 verses, many in the long triṣṭubh metre, and is often called the dramatic climax of the Gītā.",
 "<i>Mad-anugrahāya</i>, “out of grace to me”. <i>Paramaṃ guhyam adhyātma-saṃjñitam</i>, “the supreme secret called adhyātma”. <i>Moho’yaṃ vigato mama</i>, “this delusion of mine is gone”. Śaṅkara says the delusion removed is the idea that Arjuna would be the killer and his elders the killed. Rāmānuja says it is the delusion about the Lord’s nature.",
 "Name what has become clearer for you lately, and give thanks for it, before reaching for the next thing.")
DEEP[2]=("<i>Bhavāpyayau hi bhūtānāṃ</i>, “the origin and dissolution of beings”. <i>Śrutau vistaraśo mayā</i>, “have been heard by me in detail”. <i>Māhātmyam api cāvyayam</i>, “and your imperishable greatness”.",
 "The epithet <i>kamala-patrākṣa</i>, lotus-petal-eyed, is a classic description of Viṣṇu and Kṛṣṇa in hymns and temple images. Arjuna summarises chapters 7–10: the Lord as origin and end (7.6, 9.7–8, 10.8) and his greatness (10.19–42).",
 "Summarise what you have learned before asking for more. It shows you where you really stand.")
DEEP[3]=("<i>Evam etad yathāttha tvam</i>, “it is as you have said”. <i>Draṣṭum icchāmi te rūpam</i>, “I wish to see your form”. <i>Aiśvaraṃ puruṣottama</i>, “sovereign, Supreme Person”.",
 "In the Udyoga Parva, Kṛṣṇa had already shown a cosmic form in the Kaurava court when Duryodhana tried to seize him; the blind Dhṛtarāṣṭra was briefly given sight to see it. Arjuna asks for a fuller vision here. Rāmānuja notes that Arjuna’s wish comes from love, the longing to see the beloved in full glory.",
 "Let your longing to experience the truth grow from trust, not from suspicion.")
DEEP[4]=("<i>Manyase yadi tac chakyaṃ</i>, “if you think it possible”. <i>Mayā draṣṭum iti prabho</i>, “for me to see, Lord”. <i>Tato me tvaṃ darśayātmānam avyayam</i>, “then show me your imperishable Self”.",
 "<i>Yogeśvara</i>, Lord of yoga, returns in Sañjaya’s last verse: <i>yatra yogeśvaraḥ kṛṣṇaḥ</i> (18.78). Commentators say Arjuna’s request is a model of prayer: sincere wish, humble manner, final word left to the Lord. Śaṅkara notes that the vision depends on the Lord’s grace, not Arjuna’s power.",
 "Ask sincerely, and let go of the timing. What is meant to come will come when you are ready.")
DEEP[5]=("<i>Paśya me pārtha rūpāṇi</i>, “behold my forms, Pārtha”. <i>Śataśo’tha sahasraśaḥ</i>, “by hundreds and thousands”. <i>Nānā-vidhāni divyāni</i>, “of many kinds, divine”. <i>Nānā-varṇākṛtīni ca</i>, “of many colours and shapes”.",
 "The first <i>paśya</i>, “behold”, of the Gītā was Kṛṣṇa’s in 1.25: “behold these Kurus”. That look broke Arjuna. This one will remake him. Commentators note that the forms are many, but the Lord is one: diversity is his self-expression.",
 "The world’s variety is not confusion. It is one reality showing itself in countless ways.")
DEEP[6]=("<i>Paśyādityān vasūn rudrān</i>, “behold the Ādityas, Vasus, Rudras”. <i>Aśvinau marutas tathā</i>, “the two Aśvins and the Maruts”. <i>Bahūny adṛṣṭa-pūrvāṇi</i>, “many never seen before”. <i>Paśyāścaryāṇi bhārata</i>, “behold the wonders, Bhārata”.",
 "The Bṛhadāraṇyaka Upaniṣad (3.9.1–2) counts thirty-three gods: eight Vasus, eleven Rudras, twelve Ādityas, plus Indra and Prajāpati. Yājñavalkya then reduces them step by step to one: Brahman. Commentators see the same movement here.",
 "Behind the many powers of nature there is one source. Let wonder lead you toward it.")
DEEP[7]=("<i>Ihaikasthaṃ jagat kṛtsnaṃ</i>, “here, the whole universe in one place”. <i>Paśyādya sa-carācaram</i>, “behold today, moving and unmoving”. <i>Mama dehe guḍākeśa</i>, “in my body, Guḍākeśa”. <i>Yac cānyad draṣṭum icchasi</i>, “and whatever else you wish to see”.",
 "A famous parallel is in the Bhāgavata Purāṇa (10.8): when the child Kṛṣṇa’s mother Yaśodā looked into his mouth to check for mud, she saw the whole universe there, with herself and Vraja inside it. Commentators link the two scenes: once for a mother’s love, once for a warrior’s understanding.",
 "Try imagining everything you know held as one living body. It changes how you see each part.")
DEEP[8]=("<i>Na tu māṃ śakyase draṣṭum</i>, “but you cannot see me”. <i>Anenaiva sva-cakṣuṣā</i>, “with these eyes of yours”. <i>Divyaṃ dadāmi te cakṣuḥ</i>, “I give you a divine eye”. <i>Paśya me yogam aiśvaram</i>, “behold my sovereign yoga”.",
 "<i>Yogam aiśvaram</i>, “sovereign power”, was first used in 9.5. Commentators note that the gift of sight comes before the vision, and the vision depends on it. Madhusūdana says the divine eye is supernatural perception, granted only for this occasion. Spiritual traditions often speak of an inner or “third” eye.",
 "Some truths need new eyes. Ask to see with more love and clarity, not only more information.")
DEEP[9]=("<i>Evam uktvā tato rājan</i>, “having spoken thus, O king”. <i>Mahā-yogeśvaro hariḥ</i>, “Hari, the great Lord of yoga”. <i>Darśayām āsa pārthāya</i>, “showed to Pārtha”. <i>Paramaṃ rūpam aiśvaram</i>, “his supreme sovereign form”.",
 "Sañjaya now narrates what Arjuna sees, verses 9–14, before Arjuna himself speaks. Sañjaya too saw the vision through Vyāsa’s gift, and he will say at the end (18.77) that he still rejoices remembering it. The name <i>Hari</i> is beloved in devotion: “Hari Oṃ”, “Hari bol”.",
 "Even if you cannot see something yourself, listen when someone describes the light.")
DEEP[10]=("<i>Aneka-vaktra-nayanam</i>, “with many mouths and eyes”. <i>Anekādbhuta-darśanam</i>, “many wondrous sights”. <i>Aneka-divyābharaṇaṃ</i>, “many divine ornaments”. <i>Divyānekodyatāyudham</i>, “many divine weapons raised”.",
 "The Puruṣa Sūkta (Ṛg Veda 10.90.1) opens with <i>sahasra-śīrṣā puruṣaḥ sahasrākṣaḥ sahasra-pāt</i>, “the Person has a thousand heads, a thousand eyes, a thousand feet”. Commentators see this vision as that hymn come alive. The ornaments and weapons are those of a king and warrior: beauty and power together.",
 "When words fail before something vast, that failure itself points to its greatness.")
DEEP[11]=("<i>Divya-mālyāmbara-dharaṃ</i>, “wearing divine garlands and garments”. <i>Divya-gandhānulepanam</i>, “anointed with divine fragrance”. <i>Sarvāścarya-mayaṃ devam</i>, “the all-wondrous God”. <i>Anantaṃ viśvato-mukham</i>, “infinite, facing every direction”.",
 "Commentators note that the form is adorned like a temple image at festival time, in garlands, silk and sandal paste. This links the cosmic vision to daily worship. Rāmānuja stresses the Lord’s beauty, <i>saundarya</i>, as one of his qualities alongside power.",
 "Let reverence include delight. The sacred is beautiful as well as great.")
DEEP[12]=("<i>Divi sūrya-sahasrasya</i>, “of a thousand suns in the sky”. <i>Bhaved yugapad utthitā</i>, “if it rose at once”. <i>Yadi bhāḥ sadṛśī sā syād</i>, “that light might resemble”. <i>Bhāsas tasya mahātmanaḥ</i>, “the splendour of that great Being”.",
 "The Gītā uses the conditional, “if…, it might be like”: even the image of a thousand suns falls short. The Muṇḍaka Upaniṣad (2.2.10) says that the sun, moon and stars do not shine there; by its light all this shines. The Gītā repeats this in 15.6 and 15.12.",
 "Some realities outshine every comparison. Let wonder stay wonder.")
DEEP[13]=("<i>Tatraikasthaṃ jagat kṛtsnaṃ</i>, “there, the whole universe in one place”. <i>Pravibhaktam anekadhā</i>, “divided in many ways”. <i>Apaśyad deva-devasya</i>, “he saw, in the God of gods”. <i>Śarīre pāṇḍavas tadā</i>, “in the body, Pāṇḍava then”.",
 "<i>Pravibhaktam anekadhā</i>, “divided in many ways”: the universe does not dissolve into a blur; its parts remain. Rāmānuja finds here support for his view that the world is real as the Lord’s body. Śaṅkara sees the many as appearances within the one. Both readings honour unity and diversity.",
 "Unity does not erase differences. It holds them in one embrace.")
DEEP[14]=("<i>Tataḥ sa vismayāviṣṭo</i>, “then he, filled with wonder”. <i>Hṛṣṭa-romā dhanañjayaḥ</i>, “Dhanañjaya, hair on end”. <i>Praṇamya śirasā devaṃ</i>, “bowing his head to the God”. <i>Kṛtāñjalir abhāṣata</i>, “with folded hands, spoke”.",
 "<i>Añjali</i>, the joined palms, is the gesture of reverence still used in India as <i>namaste</i>. <i>Vismaya</i>, wonder, recurs in Sañjaya’s last words (18.77). Commentators say true vision always produces humility: Arjuna, the proud warrior, bows his head.",
 "Wonder makes us humble. Let your body follow your heart: bow when moved.")
DEEP[15]=("<i>Paśyāmi devāṃs tava deva dehe</i>, “I see the gods in your body, O God”. <i>Sarvāṃs tathā bhūta-viśeṣa-saṅghān</i>, “and all hosts of beings”. <i>Brahmāṇam īśaṃ kamalāsana-stham</i>, “Brahmā the Lord, on his lotus seat”. <i>Ṛṣīṃś ca sarvān uragāṃś ca divyān</i>, “all sages and divine serpents”.",
 "Arjuna’s hymn, verses 15–31, is among the finest in Sanskrit. Some commentators read <i>īśam</i> separately as Śiva, so that Brahmā and Śiva both appear within the form; most read it as a title of Brahmā. Śaṅkara takes it as Brahmā, lord of creatures. The idea that the gods dwell in one supreme being is old, going back to Ṛg Veda 1.164.46.",
 "The highest powers we can imagine are still part of something greater. That is both humbling and reassuring.")
DEEP[16]=("<i>Aneka-bāhūdara-vaktra-netraṃ</i>, “with countless arms, bellies, mouths, eyes”. <i>Paśyāmi tvāṃ sarvato’nanta-rūpam</i>, “I see you, endless in form everywhere”. <i>Nāntaṃ na madhyaṃ na punas tavādiṃ</i>, “no end, no middle, no beginning”. <i>Paśyāmi viśveśvara viśva-rūpa</i>, “do I see, Lord of all, universal form”.",
 "<i>Viśvarūpa</i>, the universal form, gives the chapter its name. In 10.20 and 10.32 Kṛṣṇa said “I am the beginning, middle and end”; now Arjuna sees that there is none. Śaṅkara says there is no limit because the Lord is infinite. Rāmānuja says every part of the world is part of the Lord’s form. Hindu art often shows the Viśvarūpa with many heads and arms, filled with gods, worlds and beings.",
 "Some things cannot be measured. Let the infinite remain infinite in your imagination.")
DEEP[17]=("<i>Kirīṭinaṃ gadinaṃ cakriṇaṃ ca</i>, “with crown, mace and discus”. <i>Tejo-rāśiṃ sarvato dīptimantam</i>, “a mass of radiance, glowing everywhere”. <i>Paśyāmi tvāṃ durnirīkṣyaṃ samantād</i>, “I see you, hard to behold on every side”. <i>Dīptānalārka-dyutim aprameyam</i>, “blazing like fire and sun, immeasurable”.",
 "The crown, mace (<i>gadā</i>) and discus (<i>cakra</i>) are Viṣṇu’s emblems; with the conch and lotus they make his classic four-armed form. Arjuna recognises them in the blaze. Commentators note the comfort in this: amid the overwhelming, a familiar sign. In 11.46 Arjuna will ask to see the crowned, mace-bearing form again.",
 "Even in the overwhelming, look for the familiar face. It is often there.")
DEEP[18]=("<i>Tvam akṣaraṃ paramaṃ veditavyaṃ</i>, “you are the supreme Imperishable to be known”. <i>Tvam asya viśvasya paraṃ nidhānam</i>, “the supreme resting place of this universe”. <i>Tvam avyayaḥ śāśvata-dharma-goptā</i>, “the imperishable guardian of eternal dharma”. <i>Sanātanas tvaṃ puruṣo mato me</i>, “you are the eternal Person, I believe”.",
 "<i>Śāśvata-dharma-goptā</i>, guardian of eternal dharma. This phrase is one source of the name <i>sanātana dharma</i>, “eternal dharma”, that many Hindus use for their tradition. Arjuna draws on terms from earlier chapters: <i>akṣara</i> (8.3), <i>nidhāna</i> (9.18), <i>puruṣa</i> (8.8). Rāmānuja notes Arjuna’s understanding growing even as his awe grows.",
 "Behind the world’s moral order stands a guardian. Let that give you courage to do right.")
DEEP[19]=("<i>Anādi-madhyāntam ananta-vīryam</i>, “without beginning, middle or end, of infinite power”. <i>Ananta-bāhuṃ śaśi-sūrya-netram</i>, “of countless arms, moon and sun as eyes”. <i>Paśyāmi tvāṃ dīpta-hutāśa-vaktraṃ</i>, “I see you, with blazing fire as mouth”. <i>Sva-tejasā viśvam idaṃ tapantam</i>, “heating this universe with your radiance”.",
 "The Muṇḍaka Upaniṣad (2.1.4) says of the Supreme: fire is his head, the sun and moon his eyes, the directions his ears, the Vedas his speech. Commentators link this to the Puruṣa Sūkta (10.90.13): the moon was born from his mind, the sun from his eye. The heat, <i>tapas</i>, that warms the world is also the power that destroys it.",
 "The sun and moon you see each day can be a reminder: you live inside something vast and alive.")
DEEP[20]=("<i>Dyāv-āpṛthivyor idam antaraṃ hi</i>, “the space between heaven and earth”. <i>Vyāptaṃ tvayaikena diśaś ca sarvāḥ</i>, “and all directions are filled by you alone”. <i>Dṛṣṭvādbhutaṃ rūpam ugraṃ tavedaṃ</i>, “seeing this wondrous, fierce form”. <i>Loka-trayaṃ pravyathitaṃ mahātman</i>, “the three worlds tremble, great soul”.",
 "<i>Adbhuta</i> and <i>ugra</i>, wondrous and fierce: the vision now turns. Rudolf Otto, the scholar of religion, cited this chapter in describing the sacred as a mystery that both terrifies and fascinates. Many traditions combine the gentle and the fierce in the Divine: Kālī and Durgā beside Gaurī, Narasiṃha beside Kṛṣṇa.",
 "Reality is not only gentle. Facing its power honestly is part of growing up.")
DEEP[21]=("<i>Amī hi tvāṃ sura-saṅghā viśanti</i>, “these hosts of gods enter you”. <i>Kecid bhītāḥ prāñjalayo gṛṇanti</i>, “some, afraid, praise with folded hands”. <i>Svastīty uktvā maharṣi-siddha-saṅghāḥ</i>, “saying ‘svasti’, hosts of sages and siddhas”. <i>Stuvanti tvāṃ stutibhiḥ puṣkalābhiḥ</i>, “praise you with abundant hymns”.",
 "<i>Svasti</i>, from <i>su</i>, good, and <i>asti</i>, it is: “may it be well”. The Vedic peace chant <i>svasti na indro vṛddha-śravāḥ</i> opens many recitations. Commentators note two responses: the gods are afraid, the sages bless. Wisdom meets awe with goodwill.",
 "In any storm, a simple blessing, “may all be well”, steadies the heart.")
DEEP[22]=("<i>Rudrādityā vasavo ye ca sādhyā</i>, “the Rudras, Ādityas, Vasus and Sādhyas”. <i>Viśve’śvinau marutaś coṣmapāś ca</i>, “Viśvedevas, Aśvins, Maruts and ancestors”. <i>Gandharva-yakṣāsura-siddha-saṅghā</i>, “hosts of gandharvas, yakṣas, asuras, siddhas”. <i>Vīkṣante tvāṃ vismitāś caiva sarve</i>, “all gaze at you in amazement”.",
 "The Sādhyas and Viśvedevas are Vedic groups of deities; the Sādhyas are said to be ancient perfected beings. Commentators note that every kind of being is included, divine, semi-divine, human ancestors and demons. The Lord is not the god of one group but of all.",
 "Before the infinite, all our divisions fall away. Remember that when you feel set apart from others.")
DEEP[23]=("<i>Rūpaṃ mahat te bahu-vaktra-netraṃ</i>, “your great form, many-mouthed and many-eyed”. <i>Mahā-bāho bahu-bāhūru-pādam</i>, “mighty-armed one, many arms, thighs and feet”. <i>Bahūdaraṃ bahu-daṃṣṭrā-karālaṃ</i>, “many bellies, terrible with many teeth”. <i>Dṛṣṭvā lokāḥ pravyathitās tathāham</i>, “seeing it, the worlds tremble, and so do I”.",
 "<i>Daṃṣṭrā-karāla</i>, “terrible with tusks”: the image recalls fierce deities like Narasiṃha or Kālī, whose fearsome forms protect devotees and destroy evil. Arjuna, the bravest warrior, is honest about his fear. Commentators find this admirable. Rāmānuja says the fierce form is shown so that Arjuna will know the Lord as the destroyer of the Kaurava host.",
 "Admitting fear is honest, not weak. Even Arjuna said, “and so do I”.")
DEEP[24]=("<i>Nabhaḥ-spṛśaṃ dīptam aneka-varṇaṃ</i>, “touching the sky, blazing in many colours”. <i>Vyāttānanaṃ dīpta-viśāla-netram</i>, “mouths wide open, eyes huge and flaming”. <i>Dṛṣṭvā hi tvāṃ pravyathitāntarātmā</i>, “seeing you, my inner self trembles”. <i>Dhṛtiṃ na vindāmi śamaṃ ca viṣṇo</i>, “I find neither courage nor peace, Viṣṇu”.",
 "<i>Dhṛti</i>, steadiness, and <i>śama</i>, calm, are the very qualities the Gītā has praised (6.25, 6.3). Arjuna loses both. Commentators see a lesson: a vision of the Divine’s power alone can overwhelm. What steadies is love and grace, which is why Kṛṣṇa will soon return to his gentle form (11.50).",
 "When everything shakes, find what you truly trust. That is your real refuge.")
DEEP[25]=("<i>Daṃṣṭrā-karālāni ca te mukhāni</i>, “your mouths, terrible with teeth”. <i>Dṛṣṭvaiva kālānala-sannibhāni</i>, “seeing them like the fire of time’s end”. <i>Diśo na jāne na labhe ca śarma</i>, “I lose my bearings and find no shelter”. <i>Prasīda deveśa jagan-nivāsa</i>, “be gracious, Lord of gods, home of the world”.",
 "<i>Kālānala</i>, the fire of time’s end, burns the worlds at <i>pralaya</i>, the cosmic dissolution. <i>Prasīda</i>, “be gracious” or “be pleased”, repeats in 11.31 and 11.45. Commentators note the paradox: Arjuna takes refuge from the Lord in the Lord. <i>Jagan-nivāsa</i>, “home of the world”: even the terrifying form is home.",
 "When life frightens you, turn toward the sacred for refuge, not away from it.")
DEEP[26]=("<i>Amī ca tvāṃ dhṛtarāṣṭrasya putrāḥ</i>, “and these sons of Dhṛtarāṣṭra”. <i>Sarve sahaivāvanipāla-saṅghaiḥ</i>, “all, with hosts of kings”. <i>Bhīṣmo droṇaḥ sūta-putras tathāsau</i>, “Bhīṣma, Droṇa and that charioteer’s son”. <i>Sahāsmadīyair api yodha-mukhyaiḥ</i>, “with our chief warriors too”.",
 "Sañjaya is reporting this to Dhṛtarāṣṭra himself; the blind king hears that his sons were seen entering death. Arjuna sees Bhīṣma, Droṇa and Karṇa, the three he feared most, already inside the Lord’s mouths. Karṇa’s secret birth as Kuntī’s son is revealed to the Pāṇḍavas only after his death. The verse shows warriors of “our side” too: the war will spare almost no one.",
 "The future is already moving. Our part is to do our duty well within it.")
DEEP[27]=("<i>Vaktrāṇi te tvaramāṇā viśanti</i>, “they rush into your mouths”. <i>Daṃṣṭrā-karālāni bhayānakāni</i>, “terrible with teeth, fearsome”. <i>Kecid vilagnā daśanāntareṣu</i>, “some caught between the teeth”. <i>Sandṛśyante cūrṇitair uttamāṅgaiḥ</i>, “are seen with heads crushed”.",
 "The Mahābhārata’s war books describe the eighteen days in great detail, and the Strī Parva describes the grief that follows. The Gītā’s vision compresses all of it into one image. Commentators read it as time devouring all bodies. <i>Tvaramāṇāḥ</i>, “hurrying”, is striking: the warriors rush toward their end.",
 "All bodies pass. Seeing that clearly can make us gentler and wiser with the time we have.")
DEEP[28]=("<i>Yathā nadīnāṃ bahavo’mbu-vegāḥ</i>, “as the many torrents of rivers”. <i>Samudram evābhimukhā dravanti</i>, “rush toward the ocean”. <i>Tathā tavāmī nara-loka-vīrā</i>, “so these heroes of the human world”. <i>Viśanti vaktrāṇy abhivijvalanti</i>, “enter your blazing mouths”.",
 "Rivers flowing into the sea is a common Upaniṣadic image for merging: the Muṇḍaka (3.2.8) says that as rivers lose name and form in the sea, the knower merges in the Supreme. The same image here shows death. Commentators note the double meaning: the sea that brings peace to the wise is fire to the bound.",
 "The same reality can be peace or fear depending on how we meet it. Prepare to meet it with peace.")
